{"parentUuid":null,"logicalParentUuid":"a6743671-47b3-4f45-bc44-95059cebbb1f","isSidechain":false,"type":"system","subtype":"compact_boundary","content":"Conversation compacted","level":"info","compactMetadata":{"trigger":"manual","preTokens":858901,"postTokens":17241,"cumulativeDroppedTokens":841660,"durationMs":59266,"preCompactDiscoveredTools":["Monitor","TaskStop","mcp__cmux-cua__bring_to_front","mcp__cmux-cua__click","mcp__cmux-cua__end_session","mcp__cmux-cua__get_window_state","mcp__cmux-cua__hotkey","mcp__cmux-cua__list_apps","mcp__cmux-cua__list_windows","mcp__cmux-cua__press_key","mcp__cmux-cua__start_session","mcp__cmux-cua__type_text"],"preservedSegment":{"headUuid":"d6019294-9609-4c23-868f-be577043df78","anchorUuid":"5735bb6a-3ccf-46cf-8887-1aa1f0e3efc9","tailUuid":"a6743671-47b3-4f45-bc44-95059cebbb1f"},"preservedMessages":{"anchorUuid":"5735bb6a-3ccf-46cf-8887-1aa1f0e3efc9","uuids":["d6019294-9609-4c23-868f-be577043df78","661e1963-a862-471c-87e8-054565e571aa","d57794a5-b0f4-4f43-852f-5057fc770be4","c15ef147-f811-4139-ae96-4e29b3de203b","876626c8-f365-4512-8a64-2f82ab0066d4","a6743671-47b3-4f45-bc44-95059cebbb1f"],"allUuids":["d6019294-9609-4c23-868f-be577043df78","661e1963-a862-471c-87e8-054565e571aa","d57794a5-b0f4-4f43-852f-5057fc770be4","c15ef147-f811-4139-ae96-4e29b3de203b","0bd0d516-99df-4378-80d5-ecbdfeab0d3f","876626c8-f365-4512-8a64-2f82ab0066d4","a6743671-47b3-4f45-bc44-95059cebbb1f"]}},"uuid":"8d4c7edd-a54a-4b3e-b834-117d130d7b34","timestamp":"2026-10-31T05:14:17.480Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/tacoda/tacoda/bastion","sessionId":"135a2369-1f42-433b-aacc-5c1b0ce18b48","version":"2.1.285","gitBranch":"main","slug":"pure-pondering-goose"}
{"parentUuid":"8d4c7edd-a54a-4b3e-b834-117d130d7b34","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"9dea46be-2d44-4b99-90f0-47176abb1cd4","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":"This session is being continued from a previous conversation that ran out of context. The summary below covers the earlier portion of the conversation.\n\nSummary:\n1. Primary Request and Intent:\n   - Rename the project to bastion, set the remote to `tacoda_github:tacoda/bastion.git`, and commit and push. Done.\n   - Enable Entire for Claude Code and pi, then commit and push. Done.\n   - Revise the plan into a Tauri + Leptos native app on the iii engine. The engine is local by default, and a remote engine comes later.\n   - **Direction changes, in order:**\n     - The app is a factory, similar to wipp (`~/Code/wipp`), but native Rust using iii directly, with no web frontend.\n     - It started out like Antigravity, then moved to a higher level: a factory, not an editor.\n     - Drop the editor. Viewing is only needed for diffs, so no file browser.\n     - All factory business logic goes in the app, in a pure crate apart from the view and from iii, with clean seams.\n     - Reach feature parity with wipp.\n     - Pivot away from the floor and inbox views. The job (a request, like a ticket) is the main idea, designed closer to GitHub Actions and wipp's Jobs page.\n     - Bastion's main value is governance you can measure:\n       - constraints and capabilities built into harness hooks;\n       - a way to promote and demote them;\n       - a tamper-proof audit log;\n       - A/B evals that show differences in harness behavior;\n       - consistent definitions: a project has a charter (or none), and Bastion is a harness and a factory.\n     - The app must be general. The owner configures their own accounts and settings to use the processes, products, tools and policies they already use.\n   - Run each phase to a live exit, update the plan with progress, and continue.\n   - \"Looks like CI is red - fix it\". Done.\n   - Most recent: \"add the repo mise.toml and start A5\".\n\n2. Key Technical Concepts:\n   - **Stack:** Tauri 2 (2.12.5), Leptos 0.8.22 (client-side rendering, Trunk 0.22.1, Tailwind 4), `iii-sdk` 0.25.0, iii engine 0.25.0 on `ws://127.0.0.1:49160`, namespace `bastion`.\n   - **Toolchain:** `rust-toolchain.toml` pins 1.97.0. The global mise config pins 1.92 and sets `RUSTUP_TOOLCHAIN=1.92.0`. Always run checks with `cargo +1.97.0`.\n   - **Three layers:**\n     - `crates/bastion-core`: pure `step(job, event, at, dials) -> (job, effects)`; depends on serde only.\n     - Engine adapter: `src-tauri/src/engine/`, the only code that imports `iii-sdk`.\n     - View: `src/`.\n     - `crates/bastion-protocol` holds the typed commands and events.\n   - **Seams, each with a fake:** Engine, Store, Machine, Commands. `scripts/check-seams.sh` enforces them.\n   - **Stock workers used:** `harness`, `iii-sandbox`, `worktree`, `session-manager`, `database` (SQLite), `github`, `approval-gate`, `secrets`, `storage`, `compose`.\n   - **Moving code in and out of the VM:** `git bundle` carries the branch in through `content_b64` and back out through a content channel.\n   - **Harness hooks:**\n     - `pre-trigger` decisions are `continue`, `deny` (with `reason`) or `hold`.\n     - `harness::function::resolve` takes `{session_id, turn_id, function_call_id, action: \"deliver\"|\"execute\", content, details, is_error}`.\n     - `harness::stop` exists.\n   - **Checkpoint dial (D15):** `ask`, `hold` or `auto`. Review never goes below `hold`. `HOLD_SECS` is 1800.\n   - **Decisions D1–D23**, including:\n     - D18: factory logic lives in the app.\n     - D19: seams are enforced by Cargo dependencies.\n     - D20: constraints and capabilities are hooks, with ladders as data.\n     - D21: hash-chain audit with one writer.\n     - D22: A/B evals.\n     - D23: integrations are adapters.\n   - **Ladders:**\n     - Constraint: told → checked → enforced.\n     - Capability: denied → ask → allowed.\n\n3. Files and Code Sections:\n   - **`PLAN.md`:** the governing plan.\n     - Sections §1–§12 cover goals, decisions D1–D23, the repository, where each feature lives, concepts (§6.1 three layers, §6.2 seams), phases, §7.1 the parity map with wipp, verification, lessons, the owner's box, risks, and progress.\n     - Phases:\n       - A0 spikes, A1 shell and stack, A2 console, A3 jobs, A4 review and landing.\n       - A5 constraints, capabilities and audit; A6 A/B evals; A7 settings and integrations.\n       - A8 full pipeline, A9 operations, A10 learning, A11 browser agent, A12 notifications, A13 packaging.\n     - Progress: A0–A4 done, A5 next.\n     - It holds \"What A1/A2/A3/A4 found\" notes.\n     - Commit `c963b33` accidentally truncated it after the D19 row. It was restored in `853e0bd`.\n   - **`crates/bastion-core/src/lib.rs`:**\n     - Types: `Stage` (Plan, Approve, Build, Review, Land), `State`, `Job`, `Event` (includes `HoldExpired`, `LandFailed`), `Outcome`, `Effect` (Provision, StartTurn, RecordArtifact, CaptureDiff, Land, Teardown, Hold).\n     - Dials: `Dial`, `Dials` with `at()`, `HOLD_SECS`, `hold_until()`.\n     - Functions: `step`, `actions`, `steps`/`StepState`, `admit`.\n     - `brief.rs` builds briefs and reads the `INTERRUPT:` question marker.\n     - A failed land waits as NeedsYou at Land, keeping its VM. Approve retries it.\n     - 24 tests in `tests.rs`.\n   - **`src-tauri/src/factory.rs`:** the adapter loop.\n     - Provision: worktree, VM and bundle.\n     - Turns use `TOOL_GUIDE`, which covers using `sandbox_id \"job\"`, that the reply ends the turn, no `bash -lc`, and `timeout_ms 60000`.\n     - The final reply is read from `session::messages`.\n     - After a build, the diff runs `git diff origin/{branch}..HEAD` and appends a \"Not committed, so it will not land\" note.\n     - The land bundle uses `origin/{branch}..{branch}`, then `worktree::land` with `target_branch`.\n     - `hold_checkpoint` runs the hold timer, and resume picks up jobs after a restart.\n   - **`src-tauri/src/store.rs`:** `SqlStore` and `FakeStore`.\n     - Tables: workspaces, jobs (with `rev`), artifacts, dials.\n   - **`src-tauri/src/engine/{mod,iii,fake}.rs`:** the Engine trait.\n     - Methods: `call`/`call_for`, `bind`/`unbind`, `read_channel`, `on_functions_changed`.\n   - **`src-tauri/src/lib.rs`:** setup and commands.\n     - On window focus it emits an empty `job-changed`, so pages re-read.\n   - **Views:**\n     - `src/pages/jobs.rs`: Jobs page and New request.\n     - `src/pages/job.rs`: job page with a waiting panel, the diff, and steps.\n     - `src/pages/diff.rs`: diff parser and line comments, with 1 test.\n     - `src/pages/settings.rs`: profile, Checkpoints dials, Workspaces.\n     - Also `src/route.rs` and `src/pages/console.rs`.\n   - **Spikes:** `crates/bastion-spikes/examples/n7_roundtrip.rs` and `n8_gate.rs`.\n   - **`docs/levels.md`:** spike results N1–N8.\n   - **`mise.toml`** (new):\n     ```\n     [tools]\n     rust = \"1.97.0\"\n     ```\n   - **`.github/workflows/ci.yml`:** runs fmt, the seam check, clippy (app and view), tests, and view tests.\n\n4. Errors and fixes:\n   - **Toolchain mismatch:** CI failed on 1.97 clippy lints because local runs used 1.92.\n     - Fixed `useless_borrows_in_formatting`, a collapsible `if` in a match arm, and `sort_by_key`.\n     - Checks now run with `+1.97.0`, and `mise.toml` was added.\n   - **Plan truncation:** a Python edit dropped the tail of `PLAN.md`. Restored it from `c0c4be2` plus the same edits.\n   - **Landing bugs:** the bundle named HEAD; a failed land tore down the VM; `worktree::land` needed `target_branch`. All three fixed.\n   - **Bundle folder:** the bundle folder was missing (fixed with `make_dir`).\n   - **Truncated reports:** `harness::status` truncates its result, so reports now come from session messages.\n   - **Stale pages:**\n     - Overlapping page reads (fixed with a latest-read counter).\n     - The factory started after the page loaded (fixed by announcing the start).\n     - A hidden webview missed pushes (fixed by re-reading on focus).\n   - **Agent misbehavior:** stuck turns from `bash -lc`, uncommitted work, and announcing the next step instead of taking it. Addressed by the tool guide.\n   - **Dirty land target:** a land failed on `target_checked_out_dirty`, from a `__pycache__` my own pytest run left in `demo-a`. Removed it.\n   - **N8 resolve:** the field is `action`, not `mode`, and it needs `turn_id`.\n   - **Classifier denials:**\n     - `iii kill --port` was denied. The user restarted the stack.\n     - A manual `worktree::remove` was denied. A leftover N7 worktree is still registered.\n     - An `rm -rf $var` command was blocked by the safety check.\n\n5. Problem Solving:\n   - A0–A4 exits were met live, driving the window with cmux-cua.\n   - In A4, `subtract` landed in `demo-a` as `0e30e2f`. Its tests pass and its VM is gone.\n   - The `divide` job failed at land before the fixes and is in Finished as failed.\n   - A hold expiring has not been verified live; it takes 30 minutes.\n   - CI is green on `853e0bd`. Commit `e7fb375` (N8) was pushed, and its CI was not checked.\n\n6. All user messages:\n   - \"Update this project to be named bastion / Remote is tacoda_github:tacoda/bastion.git\"\n   - \"commit and push to the new remote\"\n   - \"Enable entire for this project\"\n   - \"commit and push them\"\n   - \"Enable it for pi also\"\n   - \"commit and push it\"\n   - \"Take another look at the plan. Update and refine it. The goal is to have a Tauri app that uses Leptos and provides a UI/UX native app on top of the iii engine. The engine will default to local and we will add remote later.\"\n   - \"Ask me questions if necessary.\"\n   - \"I want to implement a factory that is similar to wipp. Except it will be a native rust application that uses iii directly instead of having a web frontend.\"\n   - \"The goal is to make something similar to antigravity, but built on iii\"\n   - \"Actually, drop the editor - this is more of a factory view - view files is useful, but do not do the editor\"\n   - \"And viewing really is just useful for diffs so I don't think we need a full file view access either\"\n   - \"I want this to be at a higher level to start than antigravity - it's supposed to be a factory\"\n   - \"yes, commit, and push / Then start on A0\"\n   - \"Put all the business logic of the factory in the app separate from the view and iii calls.\"\n   - \"I want clean seams in my code\"\n   - \"Try again. Shut down the iii workers. Then rerun and commit and push\"\n   - \"start on A1\"\n   - \"Make sure the plan is aware that I want feature parity with ~/Code/wipp\"\n   - \"Update the plan with our progress\"\n   - \"Continue with the plan - rerun the tasks if necessary\"\n   - \"Is the ade the console in the browser?\"\n   - \"remove ade and start A3\"\n   - \"Update the plan / Finish live exit / Finish A3\"\n   - \"I want to pivot from inbox and floor / The main idea should be the job - that's the request or the ticket / In that way it should be designed a little closer to github actions, like the jobs page in wipp / Update the plan and continue\"\n   - (A screenshot of a Trunk build-failure screen.)\n   - \"Update the plan with our current progress and then continue\"\n   - \"The main benefit that bastion provides is providing constraints and capabilities, a way to promote and demote them, a tamper-proof audit log, A/B evals and a consistent definition of charter/harness/factory. Each project has a charter (or a lack of one). Bastion is a harness and factory. Constratints and capabilities should be built into hooks so evals at higher levels can produce actionable results - like a difference in the harness behavior.\"\n   - \"This app should be general and allow you to configure all of your settings and accounts to use the processes, products, tools, and policies that you already use\"\n   - \"screen is free, finish the A4 exit\"\n   - \"Looks like that job is gone.\"\n   - \"Looks like CI is red - fix it\"\n   - \"add the repo mise.toml and start A5\"\n   - **Standing constraints:**\n     - No AI attribution in commits or PRs (user CLAUDE.md overrides the harness trailer).\n     - Write in plain technical English.\n     - Do not change the user's global mise config. (I only suggested options.)\n\n7. Pending Tasks:\n   - A5 (in progress):\n     - Constraints and capabilities as `pre-trigger` hook rules, with the decision logic in `bastion-core`.\n     - Ladders (told/checked/enforced and denied/ask/allowed) with promote and demote.\n     - A call at `ask` is held; the owner can retry with feedback, allow once, or stop.\n     - Charter-sourced constraints, and owner defaults when there is no charter.\n     - Repository hooks.\n     - The hash-chain audit, with verify and export.\n     - Constraints, Capabilities, and Audit pages.\n     - The live exit.\n   - Candidate first constraints from A4: commit every change, keep existing tests, leave a clean tree, and no `bash -lc`.\n   - Possibly D19 needs to allow `sha2` in the core for audit hashing.\n   - Then A6–A13.\n\n8. Current Work:\n   - Started A5. Spike N8 proved that hooks can deny and hold a call, and that the app can run an approved held call itself in the job's VM and deliver the result with `harness::function::resolve` (`action: \"deliver\"`).\n   - N8 is recorded in `docs/levels.md` under \"## A5\", and committed and pushed as `e7fb375` (\"Add spike N8: a hook denies, holds and releases a turn's call\").\n   - `mise.toml` was committed earlier, before N8.\n\n9. Optional Next Step:\n   - Continue A5, per the user's \"add the repo mise.toml and start A5\".\n   - Design the governance core in `bastion-core`:\n     - constraint and capability types with ladder rungs;\n     - a pure gate `decide(call, stage, policy) -> continue | deny(reason) | hold`;\n     - audit hash-chain functions.\n   - Then wire the existing `pre-trigger` binding in `factory.rs` to use it, with held calls released by approval (the app runs the call pinned to the VM, then delivers the result).\n   - Write the tests first. Run checks with `cargo +1.97.0`.\n   - Update the A5 progress row in `PLAN.md` as steps land.\n\nIf you need specific details from before compaction (like exact code snippets, error messages, or content you generated), read the full transcript at: /Users/tacoda/.claude/projects/-Users-tacoda-tacoda-bastion/135a2369-1f42-433b-aacc-5c1b0ce18b48.jsonl\nContinue the conversation from where it left off without asking the user any further questions. Resume directly — do not acknowledge the summary, do not recap what was happening, do not preface with \"I'll continue\" or similar. Pick up the last task as if the break never happened."},"isVisibleInTranscriptOnly":true,"isCompactSummary":true,"uuid":"5735bb6a-3ccf-46cf-8887-1aa1f0e3efc9","timestamp":"2026-10-31T05:14:17.062Z","turnPosition":{"promptIndex":24,"turnIndex":30},"session_id":"135a2369-1f42-433b-aacc-5c1b0ce18b48","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/tacoda/tacoda/bastion","sessionId":"135a2369-1f42-433b-aacc-5c1b0ce18b48","version":"2.1.285","gitBranch":"main","slug":"pure-pondering-goose"}
